{"seconds": 460.018, "paras": [{"n": 1, "start": 0.41, "end": 9.58, "text": "Right now, Johnny Depp is in cinemas as Ebenezer Scrooge. Th", "cut": 10.01}, {"n": 2, "start": 10.44, "end": 14.64, "text": "But Scrooge was made up. The men behind him weren't. And the", "cut": 15.12}, {"n": 3, "start": 15.6, "end": 26.44, "text": "One of them ate a bird that a rat had dragged out of a river", "cut": 27.02}, {"n": 4, "start": 27.6, "end": 37.69, "text": "By the end of this file, you'll know who the real Scrooges w", "cut": 38.22}, {"n": 5, "start": 38.75, "end": 48.19, "text": "Welcome to the Fiction Files. I'm your Detective. Every stor", "cut": 49.02}, {"n": 6, "start": 49.85, "end": 62.59, "text": "First, the fiction. In 1843, Charles Dickens introduced the ", "cut": 63.02}, {"n": 7, "start": 63.45, "end": 78.45, "text": "Scrooge counts his money on Christmas Eve. He keeps his cler", "cut": 78.88}, {"n": 8, "start": 79.31, "end": 84.86, "text": "Then three ghosts show him his past, his present, and his fu", "cut": 85.39}, {"n": 9, "start": 85.92, "end": 92.61, "text": "Dickens never said who Scrooge was based on. But historians ", "cut": 93.04}, {"n": 10, "start": 93.47, "end": 113.3, "text": "His name was John Elwes. Born in 1714, he inherited a fortun", "cut": 113.73}, {"n": 11, "start": 114.16, "end": 123.76, "text": "Elwes became a Member of Parliament for Berkshire, and by th", "cut": 124.19}, {"n": 12, "start": 124.62, "end": 128.11, "text": "And here's how he lived. His friend Edward Topham wrote it a", "cut": 128.54}, {"n": 13, "start": 128.97, "end": 139.11, "text": "He'd walk home through the rain, rather than pay a shilling ", "cut": 139.54}, {"n": 14, "start": 139.97, "end": 150.02, "text": "One day, he dined on the leftovers of a moorhen, a bird that", "cut": 150.6}, {"n": 15, "start": 151.18, "end": 158.21, "text": "And Topham saw him pick a wig up out of a rut in a country l", "cut": 158.69}, {"n": 16, "start": 159.17, "end": 165.19, "text": "When he rode to London, he chose roads with no tolls. His ru", "cut": 165.62}, {"n": 17, "start": 166.05, "end": 173.02, "text": "Scrooge at least had a fire. In the Elwes house, the fire wa", "cut": 173.55}, {"n": 18, "start": 174.08, "end": 187.89, "text": "Now, the darkest part. In his last years, Elwes owned more t", "cut": 188.32}, {"n": 19, "start": 188.75, "end": 198.46, "text": "That old woman was his housekeeper. One day, Elwes's nephew ", "cut": 198.94}, {"n": 20, "start": 199.42, "end": 204.98, "text": "Then they searched the attic. And they found the housekeeper", "cut": 205.76}, {"n": 21, "start": 206.54, "end": 212.79, "text": "Elwes survived. Scrooge, remember, only saw his lonely death", "cut": 213.37}, {"n": 22, "start": 213.95, "end": 218.62, "text": "And Dickens knew his story. He mentions John Elwes by name i", "cut": 219.15}, {"n": 23, "start": 219.68, "end": 230.41, "text": "Suspect number two. Jemmy Wood, a banker from Gloucester. By", "cut": 230.84}, {"n": 24, "start": 231.27, "end": 237.9, "text": "He ran his bank with two clerks, out of a drapery shop. And ", "cut": 238.33}, {"n": 25, "start": 238.76, "end": 245.17, "text": "His will caused a legal battle that dragged on for years. On", "cut": 245.7}, {"n": 26, "start": 246.23, "end": 256.83, "text": "And suspect number three. Daniel Dancer, who wore bands of h", "cut": 257.26}, {"n": 27, "start": 257.69, "end": 263.91, "text": "Dickens owned these stories. In one of his novels, a charact", "cut": 264.44}, {"n": 28, "start": 264.97, "end": 268.37, "text": "So, where did the name Scrooge come from? There's a famous t", "cut": 268.8}, {"n": 29, "start": 269.23, "end": 280.53, "text": "In Edinburgh, Dickens supposedly saw a gravestone for a man ", "cut": 280.96}, {"n": 30, "start": 281.39, "end": 293.34, "text": "It's a great story. But there's no evidence it happened. His", "cut": 293.97}, {"n": 31, "start": 294.6, "end": 295.91, "text": "But here's where the case turns.", "cut": 296.44}, {"n": 32, "start": 296.97, "end": 310.229, "text": "In February 1843, the British government published a report ", "cut": 310.659}, {"n": 33, "start": 311.089, "end": 318.049, "text": "Dickens was horrified. That year, he'd seen children working", "cut": 318.479}, {"n": 34, "start": 318.909, "end": 333.319, "text": "His first plan was a pamphlet, called An Appeal to the Peopl", "cut": 333.749}, {"n": 35, "start": 334.179, "end": 335.949, "text": "That sledge hammer was A Christmas Carol.", "cut": 336.679}, {"n": 36, "start": 337.409, "end": 350.249, "text": "Look again at what Scrooge says. Are there no prisons? Are t", "cut": 350.679}, {"n": 37, "start": 351.109, "end": 363.089, "text": "And the surplus population? That was a real argument too. Th", "cut": 363.519}, {"n": 38, "start": 363.949, "end": 374.899, "text": "In the book, the Ghost of Christmas Present shows Scrooge tw", "cut": 375.529}, {"n": 39, "start": 376.159, "end": 378.029, "text": "And now, the most personal clue of all.", "cut": 378.459}, {"n": 40, "start": 378.889, "end": 390.949, "text": "When Charles Dickens was twelve years old, his father was th", "cut": 391.379}, {"n": 41, "start": 391.809, "end": 396.359, "text": "Years later, he wrote that he wondered how he could have bee", "cut": 397.089}, {"n": 42, "start": 397.819, "end": 412.139, "text": "The boy who was cast away grew up to write the story of a ma", "cut": 412.769}, {"n": 43, "start": 413.399, "end": 415.189, "text": "So, let's look at the evidence.", "cut": 415.619}, {"n": 44, "start": 416.049, "end": 428.409, "text": "Real misers, richer and stranger than Scrooge, really did ex", "cut": 428.839}, {"n": 45, "start": 429.269, "end": 439.749, "text": "But there was no single real Scrooge. Dickens never named on", "cut": 440.279}, {"n": 46, "start": 440.809, "end": 444.809, "text": "So, how real is Ebenezer Scrooge? The evidence says\u2026 seven o", "cut": 445.589}, {"n": 47, "start": 446.369, "end": 458.669, "text": "Case closed. Tell me in the comments: which of the real Scro", "cut": 460.018}], "words": [["right", 0.46, 1], ["now", 0.78, 1], ["johnny", 0.99, 1], ["depp", 1.37, 1], ["is", 1.64, 1], ["in", 1.8, 1], ["cinemas", 1.96, 1], ["as", 2.39, 1], ["ebenezer", 2.55, 1], ["scrooge", 3.03, 1], ["the", 3.58, 1], ["meanest", 3.73, 1], ["man", 4.16, 1], ["in", 4.37, 1], ["fiction", 4.53, 1], ["cold", 5.46, 1], ["cruel", 5.74, 1], ["and", 6.08, 1], ["so", 6.31, 1], ["cheap", 6.6, 1], ["he'd", 6.87, 1], ["rather", 7.15, 1], ["sit", 7.55, 1], ["in", 7.77, 1], ["the", 7.94, 1], ["dark", 8.17, 1], ["than", 8.45, 1], ["pay", 8.73, 1], ["for", 8.96, 1], ["a", 9.18, 1], ["candle", 9.3, 1], ["but", 10.41, 2], ["scrooge", 10.74, 2], ["was", 11.12, 2], ["made", 11.31, 2], ["up", 11.97, 2], ["the", 11.97, 2], ["men", 12.14, 2], ["behind", 12.32, 2], ["him", 12.63, 2], ["weren't", 12.8, 2], ["and", 13.55, 2], ["they", 13.74, 2], ["were", 13.98, 2], ["even", 14.22, 2], ["worse", 14.45, 2], ["one", 15.61, 3], ["of", 15.79, 3], ["them", 15.92, 3], ["ate", 16.14, 3], ["a", 16.31, 3], ["bird", 16.4, 3], ["that", 16.62, 3], ["a", 16.84, 3], ["rat", 16.93, 3], ["had", 17.1, 3], ["dragged", 17.28, 3], ["out", 17.63, 3], ["of", 17.81, 3], ["a", 17.94, 3], ["river", 18.03, 3], ["one", 18.72, 3], ["wore", 18.93, 3], ["a", 19.2, 3], ["wig", 19.3, 3], ["he", 19.52, 3], ["found", 19.68, 3], ["lying", 19.99, 3], ["in", 20.31, 3], ["the", 20.47, 3], ["road", 20.68, 3], ["and", 21.31, 3], ["one", 21.37, 3], ["lived", 21.58, 3], ["so", 21.9, 3], ["cheaply", 22.06, 3], ["his", 22.49, 3], ["own", 22.81, 3], ["servant", 22.97, 3], ["was", 23.39, 3], ["found", 23.61, 3], ["dead", 23.92, 3], ["in", 24.19, 3], ["his", 24.35, 3], ["house", 24.56, 3], ["two", 25.32, 3], ["days", 25.52, 3], ["after", 25.77, 3], ["she'd", 26.16, 3], ["died", 26.26, 3], ["by", 27.64, 4], ["the", 27.78, 4], ["end", 27.96, 4], ["of", 28.15, 4], ["this", 28.29, 4], ["file", 28.52, 4], ["you'll", 28.75, 4], ["know", 29.07, 4], ["who", 29.3, 4], ["the", 29.49, 4], ["real